\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Theorem}
\title{Flux integers and their cancellation}
\date{2026-11-26}

\p{Let #{n\ge 2} and let #{U} be a unitary connection on #{F}. If #{c} is a
\vocabk{closed plaquette surface}{fdip-015V} whose plaquettes are all
\vocabk{plain}{fdip-015V}, then its \vocabk{plaquette flux}{fdip-015V} #{\operatorname{fl}(c)} is
an integer. The flux is additive in #{c}, changes sign with #{-c}, and is the same for #{U}
and for every gauge transform #{U^g}.}

\p{Let #{n\ge 3} and let #{\mathcal C} be an integer combination of oriented cubes with zero
boundary. Let #{\mathcal C'} be its part on the cubes that have a plaquette that is not plain,
and #{\mathcal C''=\mathcal C-\mathcal C'}. Then #{\partial\mathcal C'} is a closed plaquette
surface whose plaquettes are all plain, and
##{\operatorname{fl}(\partial\mathcal C')=-\sum_Qc_Q\operatorname{fl}(\partial Q),}
the sum over the cubes #{Q} of #{\mathcal C''}, with each term an integer. A term is zero when
the 6 plaquette angles of #{Q} lie in #{(-\tfrac{\pi}{3},\tfrac{\pi}{3})}.}

\proof{
\p{For each positively oriented edge #{e} write #{t_e} for its link
determinant. Reversing #{e} replaces #{t_e} by its inverse. The product
of the 4 oriented edge determinants is #{u_p}. Collect like edges
in the product over plaquettes to obtain
##{\prod_pu_p^{c_p}=\prod_et_e^{(\partial c)(e)}=1.}
The last equality uses #{\partial c=0}. On the support of #{c},
#{u_p=\ue^{\ui\varphi_p}}. Hence the real sum #{\sum_pc_p\varphi_p}
lies in #{2\pi\mathbb Z}, proving integrality. The sum is linear
in the coefficients, giving additivity and the reversal sign. In a
gauge transformation the intermediate site factors cancel around
the plaquette, leaving conjugation at its base point in the
[plaquette holonomy](fdip-006N). Its determinant and principal angle are unchanged.}

\p{The identity #{\partial\mathcal C'=-\partial\mathcal C''} holds
coefficient by coefficient. Every face occurring in the right side
belongs to a cube with only plain faces. Each edge of a cube occurs
twice with opposite signs in the boundaries of its faces, so
#{\partial\partial Q=0}. It follows that #{\partial\mathcal C'} is
closed and supported on plain faces. For every cube of #{\mathcal C''},
the first part gives an integer on #{\partial Q}. Sum these integers
with their coefficients and reverse the sign to get the displayed
identity. When all 6 angles have absolute value less than #{\tfrac{\pi}{3}},
their oriented sum has absolute value less than #{2\pi}. Its only
possible multiple of #{2\pi} is zero.}
}

\p{In a chosen basis, \leanref{Matrix.det_mul} gives multiplicativity
of the determinant. Mathlib's \leanref{Complex.exp_eq_one_iff} gives
the integral periods of the complex exponential.}
